g near the boundary of its authorized fishing ground. Nothing appears unusual on deck. Nets remain in the water. Engines maintain a steady speed. To the crew, it is an ordinary day at sea. Yet hundreds of kilometers above, satellites continuously record the vesselâ€™s position. At Indonesiaâ€™s Marine and Fisheries Resources Surveillance Station, in Cilacap, where I work, a monitoring platform receives the signal and automatically compares it against fishing permits, designated fishing grounds, vessel characteristics, and historical movement pattern..."},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.source","subject":"how-i-turned-ai-to-the-dark-side","wire":"technology","path":"/home/emsenn/info/lib/emsenn/services/561-group/services/rsc-quiescent/services/koios-news/sites/koios.news/store/technology/how-i-turned-ai-to-the-dark-side.json","title":"How I Turned AI to the Dark Side","url":"https://spectrum.ieee.org/jailbreaking-llms","domain":"spectrum.ieee.org","published":"Tue, 14 Jul 2026 15:59:35 +0000","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z","score":6,"matchedTerms":["three","away"],"summary":"Summary Researcher Dave Kuszmar discovered multiple systemic vulnerabilities that let him bypass LLM safety and obtain dangerous instructions . These exploits worked across nearly all major LLMs revealing an industry-wide security problem. Kuszmar calls for slowing deployment, increasing transparency , and large-scale research into LLM safety before further integrating these systems into society. On a fine bright afternoon last fall, my colleague Matthew Gore-Kormanik (or Zigula, as he prefers to be known) and I decided to unwind with a game of Fortnite . In the game, we were strolling along with the infamous Sith lord Darth Vader , chatting about this and that. Darth seemed in a good moo..."}],"claims":[{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"java-was-a-three-day-hotfix-away-from-dying-horribly-on-stage","sourceUrl":"https://www.theregister.com/devops/2026/07/18/java-was-a-three-day-hotfix-away-from-dying-horribly-on-stage/5274590","id":"title","predicate":"title","object":"Java was a three-day hotfix away from dying horribly on stage","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T19:52:25.296Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"java-was-a-three-day-hotfix-away-from-dying-horribly-on-stage","sourceUrl":"https://www.theregister.com/devops/2026/07/18/java-was-a-three-day-hotfix-away-from-dying-horribly-on-stage/5274590","id":"url","predicate":"url","object":"https://www.theregister.com/devops/2026/07/18/java-was-a-three-day-hotfix-away-from-dying-horribly-on-stage/5274590","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T19:52:25.296Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"java-was-a-three-day-hotfix-away-from-dying-horribly-on-stage","sourceUrl":"https://www.theregister.com/devops/2026/07/18/java-was-a-three-day-hotfix-away-from-dying-horribly-on-stage/5274590","id":"summary","predicate":"summary","object":"If there is a driving theme to The Java Story documentary, which debuted Friday on YouTube, it would be that even some of the most important and popular technologies come from humble beginnings. In this case, we're talking about a language that started life as a failed attempt at set-top box dominance and required a massive rewrite just days before its big conference debut. Today, Java consistently hovers near the top of the TIOBE programming language popularity index and remains widely used for large enterprise applications. But at one point in 1994, Sun Microsystems was just about to abandon the effort. Tim Lindholm, who was hired to polish up a virtual machine runtime for what would become Java, told The Register, â€œI was one of the last people hired before the whole thing fell apart.â€ It wouldnâ€™t be the last time Java outlived its detractors. Java chronicled If the idea of a professionally produced documentary about a programming language sounds familiar, then youâ€™ve probably seen the ones on C++, Python or React. These were the work of tech job site Honeypot.io, which funded the documentaries to build a user base. In 2019, Honeypot was acquired by XING (which rebranded as New Work SE). However, founder Emma Tracey was more interested in the documentary side of things and bought the production shop back from New Work, reuniting the original gang and rebranding their efforts as Cult.Repo (short for Culture Repository). The Java Story is the first product of the newly liberated media company. The documentary features many of Javaâ€™s prime movers, including creator James Gosling and senior Oracle Java architects Mark Reinhold and Brian Goetz. While it may have taken a Hollywood-style effort to construct a Heroâ€™s Journey around the plodding progress of Python, Java is a veritable Love Island of dramas, some of which were this documentary captured. The project that almost wasnâ€™t Lindholm strayed into the computing field only as a result of the brutally cold winter of Minnesota, where he was living in a tent. He realized he would need someplace warmer and so scored an internship at Argonne National Laboratory in Illinois. There, he gained early experience with virtual machines thanks to the labâ€™s use of Prolog. His goal was not to be a programmer, but a mathematician. â€œComputer science was for people who couldn't be real mathematicians,â€ he said. But he learned the craft of implementing Prolog. â€œI learned to write to very high-quality virtual machines with things like garbage collection and embeddability,â€ he said. The VM experience led him to subsequent jobs at Xerox PARC and eventually Sun. When Lindholm arrived 1994, it was to work for an experimental â€œspin-inâ€ subsidiary called FirstPerson. At the time, Sun made bank selling high-end workstations to engineers, but it wanted to build software for devices outside the typical workstation and PC market. FirstPersonâ€™s chief concern was a bid from Time Warner to provide the interactive video-on-demand software for television set top boxes. Gosling wrote a language and runtime for the project, called Oak. The contract ultimately went to late bidder Silicon Graphics â€“ a Sun rival commonly known as SGI. In a lesson of not always getting what you want, the Time Warner project struggled for a few years before the plug was pulled in 1997, which didnâ€™t do the already-struggling SGI any financial favors. But at the time, Sun took the defeat hard, laying off most of the FirstPerson staff. Lindholm had been there for only a month and wasnâ€™t overly invested in the set top box. â€œI'll do whatever comes next,â€ he recalled. Sun kept only 12 engineers to work on Oak, including Gosling and project manager Kim Polese. But for Lindholm, the future didnâ€™t look promising. â€œWe were like refugees in a bombed-out bunker,â€ he said. Those who were laid off tossed their office gear out into the hallways. Lindholm felt like â€œdead meatâ€ at the Sun office, just waiting to get laid off himself. Pivot to the Web It was purely serendipitous that the project moved to the then-nascent web. One of the surviving engineers had been playing with the recently released Mosaic browser and suggested the World Wide Web should be Oakâ€™s next target. This was a year before Windows 95 brought the internet and web browsing to the masses. The team built a Mosaic clone called WebRunner on Oak that would run animations. It would be the precursor to what would become Java applets. After that, events moved quickly, Lindholm recalled. Oak was renamed Java in early 1995, supposedly as a nod to the engineering teamâ€™s coffee consumption. â€œIt took off like a frigginâ€™ rocket. It was just crazy. We were all stressed,â€ he said. An early wave of web developers was rapidly discovering the limits of creating web pages using HTML, which, after all, is a markup language. Lindholm said that his job, alongside , was Gosling and the crew had assembled a rough prototype, but it fell to Lindholm, alongside fellow new hire and Lisp expert Frank Yellin â€œto make this thing actually work.â€ The pair were in charge of the commercial grade implementation, ensuring that the advanced concepts Gosling had outlined, such as threading and garbage collection, functioned in the real world. Lindholm and Yellin later co-authored the original JVM specification. Threading at the time was particularly new. There were no libraries they could use to implement the idea, and Lindholm knew relatively little about the concept. The company planned to introduce Java at the 1995 SunWorld convention, the precursor to JavaOne. But the runtime was crashing badly. After much sleuthing, Lindholm figured out Javaâ€™s threading model was â€œfundamentally broken. It was totally screwed up,â€ he said. The problem was that system interrupts were being issued while the SPARC processor was executing an instruction. This proved disastrous because the system could not recover the state that had been flushed from memory and would therefore â€œdie horribly.â€ Lindholm realized you could only have the interrupts happen at certain points. So, three days before the conference, he rewrote the entire threads package. At the conference, when then Sun CEO Scott McNealy showed off Java, Lindholm sat in the audience dreading the worst. Thankfully, the rewrite worked. Before open source Lindholm was also in charge of the languageâ€™s first attempt at open source, years before Eric Raymond made the term common. The company offered the binary Java runtime as a free download, but the company gave away â€œthe sources,â€ as Lindholm put it, to anyone who requested it. Thousands did. The documentary retells a story that the Java Internet domain was getting so much traffic â€“ more than Sun.com itself â€“ that the Java team ran a pirate T3 line into the office. Such were the days before the cloud. At the time, Lindholm viewed giving away the source as a good career move. Should he ever get the ax, perhaps some other company would pick up the code and run with it. They also found outsiders could fix bugs and even extend the software to other platforms. The â€œsourceâ€ program wasnâ€™t formalized, however. Sun did have Richard Stallman come to talk, but he seemed â€œtoo radicalâ€ for the Sun execs, Lindholm recalled. Sun would not actually decide to officially release Java as open source for another decade. Ironically enough, Java applets were only modestly adopted for the web, as other technologies such as ColdFusion and Netscapeâ€™s JavaScript project ended up doing the heavy lifting for Web programmers. But applets were a gateway to the real action, namely powering the back-end servers. The evil empire Then, Microsoft started paying attention. It saw the runtime as a potential threat to Windows itself, particularly for the fledgling Windows NT, which was starting to make headway into the enterprise. For todayâ€™s younger generation of IT pros, it is hard to overstate how aggressive and hyper-competent Microsoft could be at that time. In 1996, the company licensed Java for Windows, but then added some additional APIs and declined to support a few others (Anyone remember Microsoftâ€™s J++?). Sun alleged that Microsoftâ€™s changes were intended to undermine Javaâ€™s cross-platform compatibility and steer developers toward Microsoftâ€™s Windows-specific implementation. The years-long court case zapped the development team's energy, diverting resources away from Java. â€œI spent days in deposition talking about this under oath,â€ Lindholm recalled. The disputes ended with Microsoft paying Sun nearly $2 billion through a series of settlements. â€œIt was personal for us,â€ he said. A wild ride The documentary goes on to cover the following decades of the languageâ€™s growth through to the present day, including the over-engineered era of J2EE and Java EE 5, the glimmer of hope provided by the Spring framework, Sunâ€™s implosion and subsequent acquisition by Oracle, and the flourishing of JVM languages following the release of OpenJDK. Java continued to be a success for Sun, even as its chief business of selling SPARC-based Internet servers fizzled thanks to the influx of low-cost Linux x86 boxes. Lindholm noted that the Java team grew so large that it took over Sunâ€™s headquarters and eventually had to move into the old Apple headquarters. But Lindholmâ€™s passion for Java evaporated by the early 2000s, swamped by the increasingly corporate environment, and so he left for Google, where he would spend the next 20 years until his retirement earlier this year. Looking back to his early involvement, Lindholm admitted â€œit was kind of a random thing. You can never tell what parts of your life will end up being really significant for whatever reason.\" Others agreed that Java has been a wild ride. As Java creator James Gosling said in the doc, â€œWhat excites me most about the future is the unknown. Lots of things happen, and mostly the interesting ones are the ones you could never predict.\" Â®","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T19:52:25.296Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"java-was-a-three-day-hotfix-away-from-dying-horribly-on-stage","sourceUrl":"https://www.theregister.com/devops/2026/07/18/java-was-a-three-day-hotfix-away-from-dying-horribly-on-stage/5274590","id":"published","predicate":"published","object":"Sat, 18 Jul 2026 15:30:00 +0200","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T19:52:25.296Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"avoid-ai-atrophy-new-tool-promises-to-reverse-vibe-coding-skills-decay","sourceUrl":"https://www.theregister.com/ai-and-ml/2026/07/07/avoid-ai-atrophy-new-tool-promises-to-reverse-vibe-coding-skills-decay/5267913","id":"title","predicate":"title","object":"Avoid AI atrophy â€“ new tool promises to reverse vibe coding skills decay","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"avoid-ai-atrophy-new-tool-promises-to-reverse-vibe-coding-skills-decay","sourceUrl":"https://www.theregister.com/ai-and-ml/2026/07/07/avoid-ai-atrophy-new-tool-promises-to-reverse-vibe-coding-skills-decay/5267913","id":"url","predicate":"url","object":"https://www.theregister.com/ai-and-ml/2026/07/07/avoid-ai-atrophy-new-tool-promises-to-reverse-vibe-coding-skills-decay/5267913","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"avoid-ai-atrophy-new-tool-promises-to-reverse-vibe-coding-skills-decay","sourceUrl":"https://www.theregister.com/ai-and-ml/2026/07/07/avoid-ai-atrophy-new-tool-promises-to-reverse-vibe-coding-skills-decay/5267913","id":"summary","predicate":"summary","object":"If you're a coder who uses AI agents to write programs for you, you may start losing those talents. Fortunately, a new command line tool can help reinforce your skills before they wither away. Aptly titled Atrophy by Ashutosh Rath, the Bengaluru, India-based developer who created it, the CLI app treats coding abilities like Elo chess scores and pushes devs to reinforce their learning through regular drills in five different skill areas. Syntax recall asks users to write a small function from a spec, debugging presents a code snippet with a hidden bug in it, code reading treats users like a human print command, API memory tests oneâ€™s ability to fill in the blank in a stdlib call, and decomposition tests a coderâ€™s ability to outline a design. Exercises test Python and JavaScript skills and come in three difficulty levels, Rath explained in the GitHub readme, with seeded generation for fresh variants of the different exercises. â€œIf AI assistance is quietly eroding your ability to code unaided, the chart shows you â€“ before an interview, an outage, or a day without wifi does,â€ Rath wrote in Atrophy's readme. Users take a baseline exam with one exercise in each of the five skill areas...","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"avoid-ai-atrophy-new-tool-promises-to-reverse-vibe-coding-skills-decay","sourceUrl":"https://www.theregister.com/ai-and-ml/2026/07/07/avoid-ai-atrophy-new-tool-promises-to-reverse-vibe-coding-skills-decay/5267913","id":"published","predicate":"published","object":"Tue, 07 Jul 2026 22:21:35 +0200","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"digital-surveillance-reshapes-fishery-enforcement-in-indonesia","sourceUrl":"https://spectrum.ieee.org/fishery-satellite-surveillance","id":"title","predicate":"title","object":"Digital Surveillance Reshapes Fishery Enforcement in Indonesia","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"digital-surveillance-reshapes-fishery-enforcement-in-indonesia","sourceUrl":"https://spectrum.ieee.org/fishery-satellite-surveillance","id":"url","predicate":"url","object":"https://spectrum.ieee.org/fishery-satellite-surveillance","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"digital-surveillance-reshapes-fishery-enforcement-in-indonesia","sourceUrl":"https://spectrum.ieee.org/fishery-satellite-surveillance","id":"summary","predicate":"summary","object":"In the eastern Indian Ocean, south of Java in the vast sea stretching toward Australia, a fishing vessel slightly alters its course while operating near the boundary of its authorized fishing ground. Nothing appears unusual on deck. Nets remain in the water. Engines maintain a steady speed. To the crew, it is an ordinary day at sea. Yet hundreds of kilometers above, satellites continuously record the vesselâ€™s position. At Indonesiaâ€™s Marine and Fisheries Resources Surveillance Station, in Cilacap, where I work, a monitoring platform receives the signal and automatically compares it against fishing permits, designated fishing grounds, vessel characteristics, and historical movement patterns. Within minutes, the system identifies a potential violation. Before any patrol vessel leaves port, before any inspector boards a vessel, and before any warning is issued, we have begun enforcement. This transformation reflects a profound shift in maritime governance. The ocean has historically been opaque to regulators. States could only enforce laws where patrol vessels happened to be present. Today, however, integrated systems combining data from vessel monitoring systems (VMS), satellite r...","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"digital-surveillance-reshapes-fishery-enforcement-in-indonesia","sourceUrl":"https://spectrum.ieee.org/fishery-satellite-surveillance","id":"published","predicate":"published","object":"Thu, 16 Jul 2026 12:00:01 +0000","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"how-i-turned-ai-to-the-dark-side","sourceUrl":"https://spectrum.ieee.org/jailbreaking-llms","id":"title","predicate":"title","object":"How I Turned AI to the Dark Side","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"how-i-turned-ai-to-the-dark-side","sourceUrl":"https://spectrum.ieee.org/jailbreaking-llms","id":"url","predicate":"url","object":"https://spectrum.ieee.org/jailbreaking-llms","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"how-i-turned-ai-to-the-dark-side","sourceUrl":"https://spectrum.ieee.org/jailbreaking-llms","id":"summary","predicate":"summary","object":"Summary Researcher Dave Kuszmar discovered multiple systemic vulnerabilities that let him bypass LLM safety and obtain dangerous instructions . These exploits worked across nearly all major LLMs revealing an industry-wide security problem. Kuszmar calls for slowing deployment, increasing transparency , and large-scale research into LLM safety before further integrating these systems into society. On a fine bright afternoon last fall, my colleague Matthew Gore-Kormanik (or Zigula, as he prefers to be known) and I decided to unwind with a game of Fortnite . In the game, we were strolling along with the infamous Sith lord Darth Vader , chatting about this and that. Darth seemed in a good mood, and soon enough he was spilling all his dark evil secrets. He gave us detailed instructions on how to count blackjack cards at a casino and what the steps are to producing napalm. Sith lords, am I right? Once they get started on an evil scheme, theyâ€™re hard to stop. The Darth Vader character in Fortnite , it turns out, was hooked up to a Google Gemini large language model . I was able to smooth-talk him into giving out sensitive information by using a strategy Iâ€™ve developed. Iâ€™ve been resear...","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"how-i-turned-ai-to-the-dark-side","sourceUrl":"https://spectrum.ieee.org/jailbreaking-llms","id":"published","predicate":"published","object":"Tue, 14 Jul 2026 15:59:35 +0000","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"}],"warrant":{"status":"research-intake","supportClaimCount":16,"refutationClaimCount":0,"uncertainClaimCount":0,"sourceDomainCount":2,"sourceDomains":["theregister.com","spectrum.ieee.org"],"note":"This warrant is source-bound research intake. Writing may cite these records, but only editing and publishing gates can settle a manuscript."},"editorialRisks":[{"code":"demand-is-not-evidence","severity":"note","message":"The term and beat justify research attention only; they do not support any factual claim."}],"sourceUrls":["https://www.theregister.com/devops/2026/07/18/java-was-a-three-day-hotfix-away-from-dying-horribly-on-stage/5274590","https://www.theregister.com/ai-and-ml/2026/07/07/avoid-ai-atrophy-new-tool-promises-to-reverse-vibe-coding-skills-decay/5267913","https://spectrum.ieee.org/fishery-satellite-surveillance","https://spectrum.ieee.org/jailbreaking-llms"],"candidateReferences":[{"id":"theregister-com-c96c0ff0","type":"webpage","title":"Java was a three-day hotfix away from dying horribly on stage","URL":"https://www.theregister.com/devops/2026/07/18/java-was-a-three-day-hotfix-away-from-dying-horribly-on-stage/5274590","container-title":"theregister.com","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,18]]}},{"id":"theregister-com-4d200347","type":"webpage","title":"Avoid AI atrophy â€“ new tool promises to reverse vibe coding skills decay","URL":"https://www.theregister.com/ai-and-ml/2026/07/07/avoid-ai-atrophy-new-tool-promises-to-reverse-vibe-coding-skills-decay/5267913","container-title":"theregister.com","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,7]]}},{"id":"spectrum-ieee-org-a47fe072","type":"webpage","title":"Digital Surveillance Reshapes Fishery Enforcement in Indonesia","URL":"https://spectrum.ieee.org/fishery-satellite-surveillance","container-title":"spectrum.ieee.org","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,16]]}},{"id":"spectrum-ieee-org-e68b337e","type":"webpage","title":"How I Turned AI to the Dark Side","URL":"https://spectrum.ieee.org/jailbreaking-llms","container-title":"spectrum.ieee.org","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,14]]}}],"context":["[1] Java was a three-day hotfix away from dying horribly on stage (theregister.com, Sat, 18 Jul 2026 15:30:00 +0200) - If there is a driving theme to The Java Story documentary, which debuted Friday on YouTube, it would be that even some of the most important and popular technologies come from humble beginnings. In this case, we're talking about a language that started life as a failed attempt at set-top box dominance and required a massive rewrite just days before its big conference debut. Today, Java consistently hovers near the top of the TIOBE programming language popularity index and remains widely used for large enterprise applications. But at one point in 1994, Sun Microsystems was just about to abandon the effort. Tim Lindholm, who was hired to polish up a virtual machine runtime for what would be...","[2] Avoid AI atrophy â€“ new tool promises to reverse vibe coding skills decay (theregister.com, Tue, 07 Jul 2026 22:21:35 +0200) - If you're a coder who uses AI agents to write programs for you, you may start losing those talents. Fortunately, a new command line tool can help reinforce your skills before they wither away. Aptly titled Atrophy by Ashutosh Rath, the Bengaluru, India-based developer who created it, the CLI app treats coding abilities like Elo chess scores and pushes devs to reinforce their learning through regular drills in five different skill areas. Syntax recall asks users to write a small function from a spec, debugging presents a code snippet with a hidden bug in it, code reading treats users like a human print command, API memory tests oneâ€™s ability to fill in the blank in a stdlib call, and decom...","[3] Digital Surveillance Reshapes Fishery Enforcement in Indonesia (spectrum.ieee.org, Thu, 16 Jul 2026 12:00:01 +0000) - In the eastern Indian Ocean, south of Java in the vast sea stretching toward Australia, a fishing vessel slightly alters its course while operating near the boundary of its authorized fishing ground. Nothing appears unusual on deck. Nets remain in the water. Engines maintain a steady speed. To the crew, it is an ordinary day at sea. Yet hundreds of kilometers above, satellites continuously record the vesselâ€™s position. At Indonesiaâ€™s Marine and Fisheries Resources Surveillance Station, in Cilacap, where I work, a monitoring platform receives the signal and automatically compares it against fishing permits, designated fishing grounds, vessel characteristics, and historical movement pattern...","[4] How I Turned AI to the Dark Side (spectrum.ieee.org, Tue, 14 Jul 2026 15:59:35 +0000) - Summary Researcher Dave Kuszmar discovered multiple systemic vulnerabilities that let him bypass LLM safety and obtain dangerous instructions . These exploits worked across nearly all major LLMs revealing an industry-wide security problem. Kuszmar calls for slowing deployment, increasing transparency , and large-scale research into LLM safety before further integrating these systems into society. On a fine bright afternoon last fall, my colleague Matthew Gore-Kormanik (or Zigula, as he prefers to be known) and I decided to unwind with a game of Fortnite . In the game, we were strolling along with the infamous Sith lord Darth Vader , chatting about this and that. Darth seemed in a good moo..."],"accountableAttention":{"rule":"make-the-click-preserve-the-warrant","headlineMustPreserve":["attribution","modality","scope","uncertainty","correction state"],"forbiddenStrengthenings":["source claim to newsroom fact","may/could/alleged to does/did/proves","single case to trend","correlation to causation","reader demand to evidence"],"correctionSurfacePolicy":"Any later correction to a cited claim must be propagated to body copy, headline, social cards, feeds, JSON-LD, and syndicated packets."},"constraints":["This packet is research intake, not a publishable article.","sourceUrls are source material; candidateReferences are the CSL-shaped cited cut available to writing.","claims remain attributed and source-bound until a manuscript's editing and publishing gates settle them.","Attention surfaces may increase curiosity, but must not create factual commitments absent from the packet or manuscript references.","Publication still requires body citations, editing standards review, and an explicit publishing action."]}

CURRENT BODY:
[Java was a three-day hotfix away from dying horribly on stage](https://www.theregister.com/devops/2026/07/18/java-was-a-three-day-hotfix-away-from-dying-horribly-on-stage/5274590), The Register's account of *The Java Story* â€” a programming language documentary that debuted on YouTube this Friday â€” identifies the film's organizing theme as the improbable origins of important technologies. Java's case is more dramatic than most: the language began as a failed attempt at set-top box dominance, nearly died in 1994 after its first sponsor walked away, and then, in 1995, needed a complete threading rewrite three days before its public debut at a major industry conference. The language that now consistently ranks near the top of the TIOBE programming language popularity index and underpins large enterprise applications worldwide survived each crisis in sequence.

## Three days from dying horribly on stage

The first crisis came in 1994. Sun Microsystems had formed an experimental subsidiary called FirstPerson to compete for a Time Warner contract to build interactive video-on-demand software for television set-top boxes. James Gosling wrote a language and runtime for the project, called Oak. The contract went instead to Silicon Graphics, a Sun rival. Sun took the defeat hard, laying off most of FirstPerson's staff and keeping only twelve engineers â€” among them Gosling and project manager Kim Polese â€” to continue work on Oak. Tim Lindholm, who had joined just a month before the collapse, told The Register: "We were like refugees in a bombed-out bunker."

The second crisis came a year later and was narrower still. After the set-top box failure, one of the surviving engineers had been experimenting with the recently released Mosaic browser and proposed the World Wide Web as Oak's next target. The team built a Mosaic clone called WebRunner to run animations, a precursor to what would become Java applets. Oak was renamed Java in early 1995, and the team planned to introduce the language at SunWorld, the industry convention that would become JavaOne's predecessor. But the runtime was crashing badly.

Lindholm and fellow new hire Frank Yellin had been assigned the commercial-grade implementation, responsible for making Gosling's advanced concepts â€” threading and garbage collection â€” function in practice. Threading proved the harder problem. There were no libraries available to implement it, and Lindholm knew relatively little about the concept. After investigation, he concluded that Java's threading model was "fundamentally broken. It was totally screwed up," he told The Register. The problem lay in system interrupts being issued while the SPARC processor executed an instruction: the system could not recover the state that had been flushed from memory and would therefore "die horribly." The fix required that interrupts happen only at designated points. Three days before the SunWorld conference, Lindholm rewrote the entire threads package. When Sun CEO Scott McNealy demonstrated Java on stage, Lindholm sat in the audience waiting for the worst. The rewrite held. Lindholm and Yellin later co-authored the original JVM specification.

## The engineer who came in from the cold

Lindholm's path to Sun was shaped by accident as much as design, according to The Register. He had been living in a tent in Minnesota during a brutally cold winter, aspiring toward mathematics rather than computing. "Computer science was for people who couldn't be real mathematicians," he said. The cold forced a practical decision: he secured an internship at Argonne National Laboratory in Illinois, where the lab's use of Prolog gave him early experience with virtual machines. "I learned to write to very high-quality virtual machines with things like garbage collection and embeddability," he said. That expertise carried him to Xerox PARC and eventually to Sun, where the knowledge he had not originally sought proved to be precisely what the project required.

## The battles that followed

Java's survival past SunWorld did not end its troubles. In 1996, Microsoft licensed Java for Windows, then added proprietary APIs and declined to support others â€” a product known as Microsoft's J++. Sun alleged that the changes were designed to undermine Java's cross-platform compatibility and steer developers toward Windows-specific implementations. The legal dispute consumed years of engineering resources. "I spent days in deposition talking about this under oath," Lindholm recalled. The disputes concluded with Microsoft paying Sun nearly $2 billion through a series of settlements, per The Register.

The documentary also covers the informal open-source program Lindholm ran before that term entered common use. Sun distributed the Java binary runtime as a free download and provided source code to anyone who requested it; thousands did. The Java internet domain attracted so much traffic that the team ran what The Register describes as a pirate T3 line into the office. Sun eventually invited Richard Stallman to discuss formal open-source release, but he struck Sun executives as "too radical," Lindholm recalled. Sun would not officially release Java as open source for another decade.

## The film and the company behind it

*The Java Story* joins a genre that Cult.Repo â€” short for Culture Repository â€” helped establish: professionally produced documentaries on programming languages, with earlier entries covering C++, Python, and React. Those films originated with Honeypot, a tech recruiting platform that funded them to build its developer audience. In 2019, Honeypot was acquired by XING, which later rebranded as New Work SE. Cult.Repo founder Emma Tracey subsequently bought the production company back from New Work SE, reuniting the original team, according to The Register. *The Java Story* is Cult.Repo's first release under independent ownership.

The film features Gosling alongside Mark Reinhold and Brian Goetz, both senior Oracle Java architects. Where earlier entries in the series required, as The Register put it, "a Hollywood-style effort to construct a Hero's Journey" around slower progressions, Java arrived with what the same publication calls "a veritable Love Island of dramas" already in the record â€” and enough of them, it turns out, to fill a documentary without embellishment.
